/-
# Rational Kronecker factors are characteristic factors (Frantzikinakis, Problem 16)

Frantzikinakis, Some open problems on multiple ergodic averages, Problem 16: suppose that `p₁, …,
p_ℓ ∈ ℤ[t]` are rationally independent (non-trivial linear combinations of them are non-constant).
Show that `K_rat(T₁), …, K_rat(T_ℓ)` are characteristic factors for the averages `(1/N) ∑_{n=1}^{N}
T₁^{p₁(n)} f₁ ⋯ T_ℓ^{p_ℓ(n)} f_ℓ` of commuting invertible measure-preserving transformations.
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Function.ConditionalExpectation.Basic
public import Mathlib.MeasureTheory.MeasurableSpace.Invariants
public import Mathlib.Order.CompletePartialOrder

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.Dynamics.RationalKroneckerCharacteristicFactorsAMR47Problem0016Wanted

/-!
Source: N. Frantzikinakis, Some open problems on multiple ergodic averages, Bull. Hellenic Math.
Soc. 60 (2016), 41-90, arXiv:1103.3808, Problem 16. UnsolvedMath record UM-04800016
(`AMR-047-0016`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Rational independence is over `ℤ` (no non-trivial integer combination of the `pⱼ` is a constant
polynomial; Kuca calls this affine independence), with arbitrary constant terms. A system is a
family of commuting invertible measure-preserving transformations `Tⱼ` (measurable equivalences) of
a standard probability space (survey, Section 1.2; Kuca, Section 1). `K_rat(T) = ⋁_{r ≥ 1} I(T^r)`
(survey, Section 5.2.2; Kuca, (2): the σ-algebra generated by the sets `E` with `T^{-r} E = E` for
some `r ≥ 1`), and `E(f | K_rat(T))` is the conditional expectation onto it. Characteristic is the
survey's equivalent form of its definition (Section 2.2), which is Kuca's Definition 1.14: if `E(fⱼ
| K_rat(Tⱼ)) = 0` for some `j`, then `(1/N) ∑_{n=1}^{N} ∏ⱼ fⱼ ∘ Tⱼ^{pⱼ(n)} → 0` in `L²(μ)`, for
complex `fⱼ ∈ L^∞(μ)`; integer powers are taken in the permutation group of `X`.
-/

open MeasureTheory Filter Topology

/-- The rational Kronecker factor `K_rat(T) = ⋁_{d ≥ 1} I(T^d)` of `T`, as a σ-algebra: it is
generated by the measurable sets invariant under some power `T^d`, `d ≥ 1`. -/
abbrev ratKronecker {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] (T : X ≃ᵐ X) : MeasurableSpace X :=
  ⨆ d : ℕ, ⨆ (_ : 0 < d), MeasurableSpace.invariants (⇑T)^[d]

/-- The multiple ergodic averages `E_{n ∈ [N]} ∏ⱼ fⱼ ∘ Tⱼ^{pⱼ(n)}` along integer polynomials, with
`[N] = {1, …, N}` and integer powers taken in the permutation group of `X`. -/
noncomputable def polynomialAverage {ℓ : ℕ} {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] (T : Fin ℓ → X ≃ᵐ X)
    (p : Fin ℓ → Polynomial ℤ) (f : Fin ℓ → X → ℂ) (N : ℕ) (x : X) : ℂ :=
  (N : ℂ)⁻¹ * ∑ n ∈ Finset.Icc 1 N, ∏ j, f j (((T j).toEquiv ^ ((p j).eval (n : ℤ))) x)

/-- Frantzikinakis's Problem 16, as proved by Frantzikinakis and Kuca: for rationally independent
`p₁, …, p_ℓ ∈ ℤ[t]` (no non-trivial integer combination is constant) and commuting invertible
measure-preserving transformations `T₁, …, T_ℓ` of a standard probability space, the rational
Kronecker factors are characteristic: if `E(fⱼ | K_rat(Tⱼ)) = 0` for some `j`, the averages of
`f₁, …, f_ℓ ∈ L^∞` tend to `0` in `L²`. -/
def rationalKroneckerFactorsCharacteristic : Prop :=
  ∀ (ℓ : ℕ) (p : Fin ℓ → Polynomial ℤ),
    (∀ c : Fin ℓ → ℤ, (∃ a : ℤ, ∑ j, Polynomial.C (c j) * p j = Polynomial.C a) → ∀ j, c j = 0) →
    ∀ (X : Type) [MeasurableSpace X] [StandardBorelSpace X] (μ : Measure X) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
      (T : Fin ℓ → X ≃ᵐ X), (∀ j, MeasurePreserving (T j) μ μ) →
      (∀ i j x, T i (T j x) = T j (T i x)) →
      ∀ f : Fin ℓ → X → ℂ, (∀ j, MemLp (f j) ⊤ μ) →
        (∃ j, condExp (ratKronecker (T j)) μ (f j) =ᵐ[μ] 0) →
        Tendsto (fun N => eLpNorm (polynomialAverage T p f N) 2 μ) atTop (𝓝 0)

/--
Resolved true: Frantzikinakis and Kuca proved that the rational Kronecker factors `K_rat(Tⱼ)` are
characteristic for these averages for every system of commuting invertible measure-preserving
transformations, and present it as the resolution of Problem 16 (Theorem 2.10 of arXiv:2207.12288,
stated there for linearly independent polynomials with zero constant terms); Kuca's survey states
the theorem (Theorem 3.13, citing [69, Theorem 2.9]) for all affinely independent integer
polynomials, which are the rationally independent ones. Source: N. Frantzikinakis, Some open
problems on multiple ergodic averages, Bull. Hellenic Math. Soc. 60 (2016), 41-90, arXiv:1103.3808,
Problem 16; resolution: N. Frantzikinakis and B. Kuca, Joint ergodicity for commuting
transformations and applications to polynomial sequences, Invent. Math. 239 (2025), 621-706,
arXiv:2207.12288; stated in B. Kuca, Joint ergodicity - 40 years on, arXiv:2603.18974 (2026),
Frantzikinakis-Kuca, Theorem 2.10 of the arXiv version (Theorem 2.9 of the published version, as
cited by Kuca): for linearly independent p₁, …, p_ℓ ∈ ℤ[n] with zero constant terms (the standing
assumption of Section 2.2) and every system, the rational Kronecker factor is characteristic, i.e.
lim_N ‖E_{n∈[N]} ∏ⱼ Tⱼ^{pⱼ(n)} fⱼ - E_{n∈[N]} ∏ⱼ Tⱼ^{pⱼ(n)} E(fⱼ | K_rat(Tⱼ))‖_{L²(μ)} = 0 for all
fⱼ ∈ L^∞(μ), which the paper says resolves the conjecture of [8, Section 1.3] (see also [14, Problem
16]); Kuca, Theorem 3.13 ([69, Theorem 2.9]): 'Any affinely independent polynomials a₁, …, a_ℓ ∈
ℤ[t] are controlled by the rational Kronecker factor', where a₁, …, a_ℓ are affinely independent if
c₁a₁ + ⋯ + c_ℓa_ℓ is constant only for c₁ = ⋯ = c_ℓ = 0 (c_i ∈ ℤ, Section 3.1.1) and controlled
means (Definition 1.14) that for every system lim_N ‖E_{n∈[N]} ∏ⱼ Tⱼ^{aⱼ(n)} fⱼ‖_{L²(μ)} = 0
whenever E(fⱼ | K_rat(Tⱼ)) = 0 for some j; systems are invertible commuting measure-preserving
transformations of a standard probability space, https://doi.org/10.1007/s00222-024-01313-w.
-/
public theorem_wanted rationalKroneckerFactorsCharacteristic_holds :
    rationalKroneckerFactorsCharacteristic

end MathlibExt.Dynamics.RationalKroneckerCharacteristicFactorsAMR47Problem0016Wanted
